WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.425
Now we look at a system from another side.

2
00:00:03.425 --> 00:00:13.457
We seek solutions of the form vector times e to the lambda t and learn to read the picture of solutions in the phase plane,

3
00:00:13.457 --> 00:00:15.006
the phase portrait.

4
00:00:15.006 --> 00:00:18.758
At the end we linearise a predator–prey model.

5
00:00:19.008 --> 00:00:27.404
If v is an eigenvector of A for the eigenvalue lambda, then v times e to the lambda t is a solution.

6
00:00:27.404 --> 00:00:35.884
In two dimensions lambda solves lambda squared minus trace times lambda plus determinant equals zero.

7
00:00:35.884 --> 00:00:40.334
The roots can be real distinct, complex, or repeated.

8
00:00:40.584 --> 00:00:46.965
The matrix has first row minus one, minus four and second row one, minus one.

9
00:00:46.965 --> 00:00:50.695
The trace is minus two, the determinant five.

10
00:00:50.695 --> 00:00:54.341
The roots are minus one plus or minus two i.

11
00:00:54.341 --> 00:00:58.982
In the complex case we take an eigenvector for one root.

12
00:00:59.232 --> 00:01:04.795
For lambda equal to minus one plus two i the eigenvector is two i and one.

13
00:01:04.795 --> 00:01:09.832
It gives the real and imaginary parts of v times e to the lambda t.

14
00:01:09.832 --> 00:01:15.772
For example, the second solution is x equals two e to the minus t cosine two t,

15
00:01:15.772 --> 00:01:18.854
and y equals e to the minus t sine two t.

16
00:01:19.104 --> 00:01:23.139
Classification rule: a negative determinant gives a saddle.

17
00:01:23.139 --> 00:01:24.917
With positive determinant,

18
00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:31.620
trace squared greater than four times the determinant gives a node with distinct real eigenvalues,

19
00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.791
while a smaller value gives a focus with complex eigenvalues.

20
00:01:35.791 --> 00:01:39.758
Equality, trace squared equals four times the determinant,

21
00:01:39.758 --> 00:01:47.144
is the repeated-real-eigenvalue case and must be checked separately according to the number of eigenvectors.

22
00:01:47.144 --> 00:01:49.401
When the determinant is positive,

23
00:01:49.401 --> 00:01:58.976
negative trace gives asymptotic stability and positive trace gives instability; zero trace with purely imaginary eigenvalues gives a centre.

24
00:01:58.976 --> 00:02:02.601
Here the determinant is five, the trace is minus two,

25
00:02:02.601 --> 00:02:08.414
and trace squared is less than four times the determinant, so we have a stable focus.

26
00:02:08.664 --> 00:02:13.412
The graph shows x and y in time: damped oscillations.

27
00:02:13.412 --> 00:02:19.593
In the phase plane the point traces a spiral winding into the origin.

28
00:02:19.593 --> 00:02:28.015
Linearising the predator–prey model gives zero trace, a centre; but that is a borderline case,

29
00:02:28.015 --> 00:02:31.598
which linearisation alone cannot settle.

30
00:02:31.848 --> 00:02:39.387
Question: if the trace is minus three and the determinant minus two, what type is the equilibrium?

31
00:02:39.387 --> 00:02:42.157
Pause the video, choose your answer,

32
00:02:42.157 --> 00:02:48.542
then read the explanation and go back to the relevant part of the lesson if needed.
